1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,076
Hi, we are team Pokermons from V I T V.

2
00:00:03,076 --> 00:00:09,527
This is DuplexRAG, our submission for theme four of the Samsung PRISM Generative AI Hackathon:

3
00:00:09,527 --> 00:00:10,831
streaming live RAG.

4
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:16,219
In a voice conversation people say one natural request, not a tidy query.

5
00:00:16,219 --> 00:00:23,550
One sentence can hide several questions, the key detail may arrive last, and users add details after they hear the answer.

6
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:30,340
Waiting for the end of speech before searching leaves dead air, and restarting on every correction loses context.

7
00:00:31,343 --> 00:00:32,963
DuplexRAG is event driven.

8
00:00:32,963 --> 00:00:39,568
Every transcript chunk goes to a retrieval controller that decides whether to wait, retrieve, or suppress.

9
00:00:39,568 --> 00:00:45,799
A decomposer splits the utterance into the searches it implies and carries shared context into each.

10
00:00:45,799 --> 00:00:53,213
BM25 and dense retrieval are fused and reranked by a small cross encoder, on the CPU, while the user is still speaking.

11
00:00:53,213 --> 00:01:01,937
The session aware composer answers every intent with cited corpus sentences, verifies every claim, and flags what the corpus cannot support.

12
00:01:03,343 --> 00:01:07,262
Here is the example from the theme guide, replayed in real time.

13
00:01:07,262 --> 00:01:17,977
The controller waits on the first words, fires a provisional search once Pune and thirty people are stable, and dispatches the cancellation and catering searches mid sentence.

14
00:01:17,977 --> 00:01:21,345
When the user stops, the answer streams almost at once:

15
00:01:21,345 --> 00:01:25,019
one line per hidden question, and a citation on every claim.

16
00:01:25,019 --> 00:01:25,876
Note the flag:

17
00:01:25,876 --> 00:01:30,224
catering for the Hinjewadi venue could not be verified from the corpus.

18
00:01:30,224 --> 00:01:31,326
Now a late detail:

19
00:01:31,326 --> 00:01:32,857
fifty people, not thirty.

20
00:01:32,857 --> 00:01:38,674
The answer becomes version two; only the capacity claim is searched again and the rest is kept.

21
00:01:38,674 --> 00:01:40,878
Finally, repeat that in two bullets:

22
00:01:40,878 --> 00:01:43,817
no retrieval at all, same citations, new format.

23
00:01:56,676 --> 00:01:58,138
The second guide example.

24
00:01:58,138 --> 00:02:01,236
A travel reimbursement question gets a cited summary.

25
00:02:01,236 --> 00:02:06,439
Then the user adds that the trip was international and the booking was made after travel.

26
00:02:06,439 --> 00:02:08,017
DuplexRAG does not restart.

27
00:02:08,017 --> 00:02:15,558
It keeps the standard rule and adds the foreign currency receipt check and the senior director approval for post travel bookings.

28
00:02:15,558 --> 00:02:19,416
The thank you is recognised as small talk, so nothing is searched.

29
00:02:30,676 --> 00:02:32,654
Here the key entity arrives last:

30
00:02:32,654 --> 00:02:34,212
oh, and it's in Bengaluru.

31
00:02:34,212 --> 00:02:44,579
Speculative searches that did not know the city are invalidated and run again, so the answer covers Bengaluru venues, catering and event safety for a hundred and ten people.

32
00:02:44,579 --> 00:02:51,470
A what if about cancelling ten days before refines the answer, and give me the gist condenses it without searching.

33
00:03:13,243 --> 00:03:20,527
We measured everything with a discrete event replay on a held out set written independently after the engine was frozen.

34
00:03:20,527 --> 00:03:25,140
Retrieval started before the end of speech in 100 percent of eligible turns.

35
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:28,661
Compound requests were decomposed correctly in 83 percent.

36
00:03:28,661 --> 00:03:37,827
100 percent of claims were supported by their citation, with zero fabricated document IDs, and 88 percent of refinements were versioned and delta only.

37
00:03:37,827 --> 00:03:41,712
The turn based baseline never retrieves early and cannot refine.

38
00:03:42,713 --> 00:03:54,919
DuplexRAG runs on a laptop CPU with two small open models, costs about a hundredth of a cent per turn, and ships with Docker, a replay benchmark, telemetry, and a live microphone demo.

39
00:03:54,919 --> 00:03:55,583
Thank you.

